from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-4454';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Theorem 5 and Section 3, equation (25): the missing expression is the square-root metric, for p>2.'
refs += [dict(id='journal',title='Snowflake universality of Wasserstein spaces',authors='Alexandr Andoni; Assaf Naor; Ofer Neiman',year=2018,url='https://doi.org/10.24033/asens.2363',pdf_url='https://www.numdam.org/item/10.24033/asens.2363.pdf',locator='Annales scientifiques de l’École Normale Supérieure, series 4, 51(3):657–700. Sections 1.1–1.2 define distortion and state Theorems 1–2; Question 23, equation (52), retains the half-snowflake conjecture.'),dict(id='later',title='Coarse Embeddability of Wasserstein Space and the Space of Persistence Diagrams',authors='Neil Pritchard; Thomas Weighill',year=2024,url='https://doi.org/10.1007/s00454-024-00674-6',locator='Published online 26 June 2024; Discrete & Computational Geometry 74:358–373 (2025). Corollary 25 and Theorem 27 concern coarse embeddings and 1/p-snowflake universality over the plane, not the half-snowflake target over R³ for p>2.')]
save(i,dict(title='Half-snowflake embeddings into Wasserstein p-space',formal=r'For every fixed real \(p\in(2,\infty)\), does there exist a finite constant \(C_p\ge1\) such that every finite metric space \((X,d)\) admits a map \(F:X\to\mathcal P_p(\mathbb R^3)\) and a scale \(s>0\) satisfying \(s\sqrt{d(x,y)}\le W_p(F(x),F(y))\le C_p s\sqrt{d(x,y)}\) for all \(x,y\in X\)? The constant may depend on p but must be independent of X, its number of points and its distances.',definitions=r'''A finite metric space consists of a finite set X and a symmetric function d:X×X→[0,∞) with d(x,y)=0 exactly when x=y and d(x,z)≤d(x,y)+d(y,z). Its half-snowflake is the metric space with distance √d(x,y). The square root applies to the original distances, not to the Wasserstein distance or to the dimension. Distances in the base space R³ are the usual Euclidean distances.

The set \(\mathcal P_p(\mathbb R^3)\) consists of Borel probability measures μ on R³ satisfying \(\int\|u\|_2^p\,d\mu(u)<\infty\). A coupling of μ and ν is a probability measure π on R³×R³ with these two marginals. Write Π(μ,ν) for all such couplings. The Wasserstein metric is \(W_p(\mu,\nu)=(\inf_{\pi\in\Pi(\mu,\nu)}\int\|u-v\|_2^p\,d\pi(u,v))^{1/p}\). Thus the transport cost uses the pth power of Euclidean distance and takes a pth root after minimizing. Images may be arbitrary measures of finite pth moment; no support-size, equal-mass, rational-coordinate or computability restriction is imposed.

The two-sided inequality means a bi-Lipschitz embedding of distortion at most C_p after a common rescaling s. It forces F to be injective when X has more than one point. Both F and s may depend on p and the entire finite metric. The quantifier order is ∀p>2 ∃C_p<∞ ∀(X,d) ∃F,s; there is no single bound required to work uniformly as p varies, and p=∞ is excluded. This is an existence question, with no algorithmic running-time target.

The source writes c_Y(X,√d)≤D_p, where c_Y is the infimum of the distortions of all embeddings. The infimum need not be attained. Existence of some finite uniform bound in that formulation is equivalent to the displayed formulation: increasing an available finite infimum bound, for example to twice its value, allows actual embeddings for each finite X. The source's stronger suggestion D_p=1 instead means distortion arbitrarily close to one for every finite metric. It must not be silently replaced by a demand for an attained isometric embedding, and neither strengthening is required here.

The already-proved theorem embeds the 1/p-snowflake, with distances d(x,y)^{1/p}, with distortion arbitrarily close to one. When p>2 its exponent is smaller than 1/2, so it does not answer this question. At p=2 it does give the half-snowflake result; that endpoint is background, outside the unresolved range.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Prove the uniform-in-X finite-distortion assertion for every fixed p>2, or refute it by finding a fixed p>2 for which half-snowflake distortions into this exact target space are unbounded across finite metrics. A single finite metric with distortion greater than one does not refute the claim. Results allowing the distortion or base-space dimension to grow with |X|, or using exponent 1/p instead of 1/2, do not settle it. No optimal numerical value of C_p is requested.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The conjecture raises the snowflake exponent',text='The source proves almost-isometric universality at exponent 1/p. Question 23 of the full journal paper asks for bounded distortion at the larger exponent 1/2 when p>2. This restores the mathematical expression lost from the saved extraction.',citation='journal'),dict(title='The constant must work for every finite metric',text='Allowing a different, arbitrarily large distortion bound for each finite metric would remove the central uniformity requirement. The proposed C_p depends only on the fixed Wasserstein exponent.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Related questions have different demands',text='The source separately raises almost-isometric half-snowflake embeddings, embeddings of the entire Wasserstein-2 space into Wasserstein-p space, and sharp lower bounds above exponent 1/2. Those are not substituted for this finite-distortion conjecture.',citation='journal'),dict(title='Recent planar results concern another setting',text='The 2024 paper relates coarse embeddability and 1/p-snowflake universality for Wasserstein space over R² to persistence diagrams. Its stated results do not supply half-snowflake universality in R³ for p>2.',citation='later')],why='Asks how richly a fixed three-dimensional transport space can represent arbitrary finite metric geometry after taking square roots of distances. The proposed exponent would improve the known universality range beyond what follows directly from the transport-cost exponent.',progress=[dict(date='2016',text='The ICALP paper proves 1/p-snowflake universality and states the bounded-distortion half-snowflake conjecture in equation (25).',citation='primary'),dict(date='2018',text='The full journal treatment retains the conjecture as Question 23, equation (52), while proving sharpness of the known exponent for 1<p≤2.',citation='journal'),dict(date='2024-06-26',text='Later work establishes planar Wasserstein/persistence-diagram relationships for coarse embeddings and 1/p-snowflake universality, a different target.',citation='later')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read the conference equation, the full journal definitions and Question 23, and the later publisher full-text results on planar snowflake universality. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no resolution of bounded-distortion half-snowflake universality for every p>2 in P_p(R³). The extraction’s apparent p before d was the square-root sign, not an exponent or coefficient.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Restored √d and p>2, defined Wasserstein measures, couplings, distortion and quantifier order. Distinguished an infimum from an attained embedding and removed the suggested W₂-to-W_p proof route.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A structural universality conjecture about transport metrics that asks for a larger snowflake exponent with size-independent distortion. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The question asks whether every finite metric becomes a bounded-distortion subset of Wasserstein p-space over R³ after taking square roots of its distances.','The exponent p is fixed above two, and the distortion bound must depend only on p.','The known theorem works at snowflake exponent 1/p, which is smaller than one half in this range.','Almost-isometric embeddings and embedding the entire Wasserstein-2 space are separate stronger questions.','The later planar results checked here do not settle this three-dimensional half-snowflake conjecture.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','journal','later'])),notes=['Recovered the missing square root and the unresolved range p>2.','Defined the exact Euclidean Wasserstein target, finite moments, couplings and uniform distortion.','Preserved the finite-constant existence target and distinguished infimum one from an attained isometry.','Checked the full 2018 journal formulation and later planar results; removed the proposed embedding route.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://web.math.princeton.edu/~naor/homepage%20files/snowflakeWp.pdf'])
